1|1@pics^1440659316#cuh866g:sangneuf$:I'm not especially motivated to draft statutory legislation for you at 2:30 in the morning because you insist on being obtuse. That said, the draft edition is this: Both exchanges include offer and acceptance by two parties, as such, whether in written or verbal form, they exist conceptually under the law of contracts. The fundamental difference is the element of conditionality, beyond offer and acceptance, that differentiates income from gifts. Income is only provided by one party to another in exchange for a service or product of value. If the service or product is not provided, the party responsible for providing the other party's income is no longer obligated to do so. Gifts are missing the element of conditionality. They create no obligation for the receiver of the gift. TL;DR There is no sex in the champagne room, precisely because a gift is offered rather than a payment for services. 
