10|0@pics^1440632581#cugvlru:keithps$:As a volunteer FF, that picture is my absolute worst nightmare. I've never had a really bad call involving a child and I hope I never do.
5|0@pics^1440641158#cuh0f7p:2BuckFuckNChuck$:Stick with it long enough and you'll see things you won't forget. When you do, learn to talk about it. You're no less of a firefighter for having feelings. I've been doing it for a little over 6 years and I already have memories that comeback to me at any given time. I've learned to cope and I talk to other guys on the job but I know eventually I'll need professional help. Don't be afraid to seek help if you need it. I'm willing to listen, if you're already getting to that point. Sometimes that's all you need. 
2|0@pics^1440646988#cuh3klz:EstellaH$:The best type of care is preventative care, be it physical or mental. Do not wait until you NEED some help or someone outside the department to talk to. I suggest finding a local therapist that either specializes in police/fire or deals with PTSD. See them once a month, talk to them, develop a rapport, talk about those hard calls that sometimes pop into your head in the middle of the night. It wi become natural and easy to talk to that person, and when ( not if ) you get a call that really throws you for a loop, you will know exactly who to call when it's messing with your head too bad and it's affecting other parts of your life ( sleep, appetite, marriage, kids, sex, etc ) . Take care of yourself, you do a tough job that not many are willing to do. You're running in to things, when the natural instinct is to run away. 
2|0@pics^1440641451#cuh0lau:keithps$:Thanks. I've also been doing it about 6 years. I've seen all kinds of horrible shit, but the only thing that bugs me are incidents with kids. I've worked traumatic child injuries, but nothing serious or fatal, just broken bones and the like. I dread the bad one involving a kid, I just hope it's a long time away.
